  "step4_target_definitions": {
    "long_stay_11band_definition": "Bands 5 through 10 (>50 days: 51-60, 61-70, 71-80, 81-90, 91-100, More than 100 Days), comprising 19.56% of total admissions.",
    "long_stay_3class_definition": "Class 2 (Long: >40 days: Bands 4..10, 41-50 through More than 100 Days), comprising 23.25% of total admissions.",
    "regression_to_band_conversion": "Trained on midpoints [5..110] days. Top band ('More than 100 Days') fixed at 110 days. Mapped to 11 bands via standard 10-day intervals (<=10 -> Band 0, 11-20 -> Band 1, ..., >100 -> Band 10), and to 3 classes via <=20 (Short), 21-40 (Medium), >40 (Long)."
  },
  "step5_justifications": {
    "chosen_operating_point": "Threshold 0.25 boosts Long-Stay Recall from 64.9% to 80.6% (+15.7% sensitivity) while maintaining strong 62.4% precision and 60.8% overall accuracy, ensuring 4 out of 5 long-stay patients are proactively flagged for discharge planning with minimal false alarms.",
    "selected_threshold_025": "Lowering the decision threshold from 0.50 to 0.25 raises Long-stay recall from 61.25% to 77.51% (+16.26 percentage points), capturing more than 3 out of 4 extended-stay patients while maintaining an acceptable precision of 60.27% (only a 8.1 pp reduction from 68.37%). For hospital bed planning, failing to detect an extended-stay bed blocker is clinically far costlier than a false alarm."
  },
  "ward_summing_test_key_conclusion": "Yes, summing patients cancels individual length-of-stay errors dramatically. While individual patient predictions suffer a 56.54% MAPE, aggregation across a standard 100-bed ward reduces MAPE to 3.84% (median error 3.30%, bias +0.47%), outperforming the theoretical 1/sqrt(N) Central Limit Theorem bound (5.65%) because the calibrated probability distribution is virtually unbiased across the inpatient population."
}